A||N||4||Soulsinger||Dead Fiero||||11||
Z||000000||Soulsinger||04-12-2000||12:07 AM||||I was driving to work and I noticed smoke coming from the engine, when I checked I saw no signs of water leaking (the temp guage was normal) I did notice that the overflow container was empty so I filled it. I also had a minor valve cover gasket leak so I wiped up the tiny amount of oil around the screws thinking it just may have been burning off some oil.  I drove another two miles and the car overheated and the temp guage when red line and then the engine shut down (I was on the freeway and had to find a safe place to pull over).  After it became light I found water coming from the water pump gasket (the water pump was just installed three days prior).  OK I know the water pump needs to be fixed, but I added some more water to get to the next exit (1/2 mile) but the car will not restart (after hours of sitting so it was not hot).  It will crank and acts like it wants to start but just won't.  It acts like it is starving for fuel but we can see the fuel dripping as it should (84 Coupe 4 Cyl.).  There is no sign of water in the oil, the plugs look good.  If anyone has any ideas your help would be appreciated.||||reg||11
Z||000001||wkayl||04-12-2000||12:39 AM||||Sounds as if you may have warped the valves.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||04-12-2000||01:36 AM||||You might want to check the temp sensor for the fuel injection.  It's the one on the thermostat housing at the transmission end of the engine.  I fried mine once when the engine was overheated severely (along with the temp gauge sensor and the radiator fan switch) and it caused the engine to barely run at all.  If you check the cylinder compression and it is OK, then you haven't hurt the valves.||||reg||4
Z||000003||theogre||04-12-2000||07:41 PM||||Overheating a 4 is not thing good to do.<P>Have the head checked, especially the 5 front side head bolts. If these are the original bolts you may have one failed. GM says the three middle ones are the worst culprits.(These are supposed to be replaced when the recall work is done but likely wheren't.)<P>If one of the head bolts has stretched or broken you can blow the head gasket. Do a compression test.<P>there is a "HOW TO:" post about the temperature sensors. It was updated a few days ago so it should be easy to find.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||04-12-2000||08:32 PM||||Hook a vacuum gauge up to a vacuum source and crank the engine with the PCV valve and breather tube blocked off. You should have 1-3 hg of cranking vacuum in a healthy engine. <P>If the engine won't turn over than pull the spark plugs to see if the engine is hydro-locked (water in the cylinders) and try to turn it by hand. <P>Make sure you have spark, fuel (you say you have), and air (checked with vacuum gauge called a cranking vacuum test). These are the three things an engine must have to start. If any of these are not present than she will not start, simple!<P>Good luke.||||reg||1
